WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:06.800
If I had to learn trading again from zero, I&nbsp;
wouldn't start with indicators, strategies, or 50&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:13.440
different chart patterns. I'd learn one skill, how&nbsp;
to look at a chart and recognize what is starting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.440 --> 00:01:45.920
to happen before everyone else sees it. By the&nbsp;
end of this, you will read a chart in five moves:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:53.280
pressure, attempt, failure, control, change, and&nbsp;
first pullback. Right now, those are just words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:58.240
but if you master this, you will know&nbsp;
what to look for before the move happens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:04.640
not after it is already gone. So, a beginner&nbsp;
opens a chart and sees individual candles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:11.920
green ones, red ones, big ones, small ones, with&nbsp;
no apparent logic connecting them. An experienced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:18.720
trader opens the same chart and sees pressure&nbsp;
building near a level, a failed attempt by buyers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:24.480
to push through. And the moment sellers begin&nbsp;
taking control. The candles on the screen are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:30.240
identical in both cases. The reading is&nbsp;
completely different. Beginners see details,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:36.720
but experts see relationships between the details.&nbsp;
And the good news, pattern recognition is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:42.960
a natural talent that you either have or you do&nbsp;
not. The brain learns this skill through exposure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:49.920
prediction, and feedback. And most important, this&nbsp;
system is not about memorizing more names. You'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:55.600
learn to recognize the relationship between&nbsp;
what price tries to do and what happens after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:03.520
that attempt. I'll show you. So why over 90% of&nbsp;
traders fail because of how patterns get taught?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:10.640
Open almost any beginner trading book and you will&nbsp;
find beautiful diagrams, two pigs exactly level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:16.080
a perfect triangle with clean touches.&nbsp;
Those pictures are useful for one thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:22.080
which is naming something after it is over. The&nbsp;
shapes look elegant on the diagram. Then you open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:28.960
a real chart and nothing matches. Look at what&nbsp;
a real one looks like. This is a 15-minute chart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:35.040
with what a textbook would call a double top,&nbsp;
except the second peak is higher than the first.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:42.320
There is a third small peak in between. One candle&nbsp;
has a wick twice as long as anything around it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:49.200
Now you are stuck wondering whether this qualifies&nbsp;
as the pattern or not and that hesitation costs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:55.440
you real money. And while you are trying to decide&nbsp;
whether the shape deserves a name, the useful part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:01.920
of the move is already happening. Price has been&nbsp;
moving higher. It reaches an obvious high and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:08.080
drops. Then buyers return and push through that&nbsp;
high for a moment. The breakout looks strong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:14.560
but price cannot stay above it. the next candle&nbsp;
slide back under the level and then selling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:20.640
becomes aggressive. You could give that sequence&nbsp;
several technical names depending on the method&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:27.360
you trade but none of those names changes what&nbsp;
happened. Buyers tried to claim higher prices.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:34.480
They failed to hold them. Sellers responded.&nbsp;
This is what happened. Real charts change three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:41.040
things every single time. Candle size changes&nbsp;
with volatility. The number of candles changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:48.160
with time of day. The speed changes with whoever&nbsp;
happens to be active. The story stays the same. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:54.480
the problem is not a pattern itself. The problem&nbsp;
is treating a pattern like a photograph that price&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.480 --> 00:05:00.800
must replicate exactly before you are allowed to&nbsp;
act. But real markets don't produce symmetrical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:07.600
textbook drawings. Candle sizes shift with&nbsp;
volatility. A formation that builds in 20 candles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:14.640
during a fast session might take 50 candles during&nbsp;
a quiet one, but the behavior underneath remains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:21.600
consistent. Price builds pressure against the&nbsp;
level, pushes through, fails, and reverses. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:27.280
sequence can look completely different in terms&nbsp;
of candle count, candle size, and the speed of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:34.160
each phase while still carrying the same meaning&nbsp;
and producing the same type of tradable move.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:40.400
So instead of figuring out what shape starts to&nbsp;
form, start asking a different question. What is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:48.000
price trying to do? Watch this high. Buyers are&nbsp;
trying to break it. Now this low, sellers keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:54.800
pushing into it. Then this range, neither side is&nbsp;
making progress yet. This will shift your focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.800 --> 00:06:01.680
from geometry to behavior because a classic chart&nbsp;
pattern is a picture. But pattern recognition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:07.920
is understanding what the picture means. And we&nbsp;
can reduce that entire reading process to three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:15.040
simple questions. Question one, where is price&nbsp;
struggling? Not where is price, where does it keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:21.840
getting stopped? On this 15-minute chart, price&nbsp;
gets stopped at the same high several times. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:27.760
my eyes go to that upper edge and nowhere&nbsp;
else. Struggle looks like repeated visits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:35.840
to one area. a tight range or a level that keeps&nbsp;
getting tested from the same side. Question two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:42.480
which side is trying to move it? Look at who is&nbsp;
doing the work here. The candles walking into that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:49.760
high are green and the retreats are shallow, which&nbsp;
tells me buyers are the ones attacking and sellers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:56.640
are just defending a line. Question three, is the&nbsp;
attempt working? This is the question that pays.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:04.080
Working means price continues cleanly and creates&nbsp;
new space. Not working means price stops making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:10.480
progress or pushes through and immediately&nbsp;
comes back or gets shoved back by the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:17.920
side. On this chart, the answer to question three&nbsp;
is no. The attempt isn't working. Several attacks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:25.360
no new ground, and the last two attempts closed&nbsp;
lower than the first. buyers are attacking and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:32.560
losing. So where is the fight? Who is attacking?&nbsp;
And is the attack working? Those three questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:40.640
can organize almost any chart without forcing you&nbsp;
to know advanced terms. Try them on this level.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:46.960
The fight is at the low. Sellers are attacking.&nbsp;
They break it with a large red candle. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:54.720
next candle makes a new low and closes near its&nbsp;
bottom. The next one continues. So yes, sellers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.720 --> 00:08:01.600
are holding below the level. There is no failed&nbsp;
attempt here. Move to another chart. Price breaks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:08.320
a low but immediately prints a long lower wick.&nbsp;
Then the following candle closes back above the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:15.680
level. Sellers attacked but the attempt did not&nbsp;
work. Here's the rule I want you to keep. A break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:22.400
matters only if price can hold beyond the level.&nbsp;
This is also where chart reading starts becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:28.480
less emotional. You don't need to guess whether a&nbsp;
breakout feels strong. You ask whether price made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:34.800
progress after the break. And the earliest warning&nbsp;
that one of these attempts is getting close often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:41.360
appears before the breakout itself. When price&nbsp;
starts running out of room, watch this zone for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:49.120
few seconds. Price reaches it, sellers push back,&nbsp;
and the pullback travels a decent distance. Buyers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:56.320
return. Price reaches the high again. Sellers&nbsp;
respond again, but the second pullback is smaller.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:03.360
Then buyers come back for a third attempt. This&nbsp;
time, sellers barely create any distance. Price&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:09.360
pulls away for several small candles, then&nbsp;
presses straight back into the same high.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:16.000
That is pressure. You can almost see the available&nbsp;
space being squeezed out of the chart. The easiest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:23.040
way to recognize it is not by counting candles.&nbsp;
Compare the reactions away from the level. If each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:30.880
reaction travels less distance than the one before&nbsp;
it, one side is losing room. The same here. Buyers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:37.760
keep returning to the same place but are producing&nbsp;
less distance. Price is spending more time pressed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:44.800
against the edge and less time retreating from it.&nbsp;
Before price moves quickly, it often runs out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:52.800
space. That narrowing range is pressure and it is&nbsp;
the first piece of the pattern worth recognizing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:58.400
So pressure gives your eyes something simple to&nbsp;
search for. You are not trying to predict the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:04.000
breakout direction from compression alone. You&nbsp;
are simply noticing that the chart is becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:11.120
more interesting. Pressure is preparation,&nbsp;
not permission to enter. Not yet. Because this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:17.600
pressure can release in either direction. The&nbsp;
chart can tighten under a high and then fail.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:24.240
It can tighten above a low and suddenly reverse.&nbsp;
Compression tells you that both sides are getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:30.400
forced closer together. It does not guarantee&nbsp;
which side wins. So when you spot compression&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:37.280
against a clear level, mark it and pay attention&nbsp;
to the next serious attempt. Look at this second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:44.320
chart. Price is pressing into a low. The first&nbsp;
bounce is strong. The second is smaller. The third&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:52.320
bounce is very weak. Candles begin overlapping&nbsp;
close to the low. Then sellers break that low and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:59.120
continue immediately. In this case, the pressure&nbsp;
released in the direction of the attack. Now this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:07.120
one, price is compressing under a high. Candles&nbsp;
are overlapping wicks on both sides. Then buyers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:13.920
finally break above the high with a big green&nbsp;
candle. But the next candle barely moves. Then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:20.320
price falls back below the marked zone. This is&nbsp;
where pressure becomes useful because it gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:26.800
us time to prepare for the attempt. It did not&nbsp;
tell us to blindly trust the attempt. The real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:33.360
information comes from what price does after it&nbsp;
crosses the line. That failure is the pattern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:40.480
I care about most. So pressure builds and then&nbsp;
somebody has to try. Watch what happens when they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:48.160
do because this is the moment the entire strategy&nbsp;
leaves or dies. Price finally breaks the high. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:55.360
candle is big. It closes above the level with a&nbsp;
strong body and it looks completely convincing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.360 --> 00:12:02.160
New buyers enter on that candle because it looks&nbsp;
like exactly what everyone is taught to buy. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:08.400
watch the next three candles. The first one cannot&nbsp;
extend. The second one closes back inside the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:15.680
level. The third one closes even lower. Everyone&nbsp;
who bought that break is now underwater and their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:22.320
stops sit right below the level they just bought.&nbsp;
When those stops trigger, they become sell orders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:29.120
which pushes price further down. That is the&nbsp;
setup, the failed break. This is one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.120 --> 00:12:35.440
useful changes you can train your eye to notice&nbsp;
because a failed attempt tells you more than a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:42.080
clean breakout. A clean breakout tells you one&nbsp;
side is strong. A failed breakout tells you one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:48.960
side tried, attracted new positions and then lost&nbsp;
control at the exact moment it needed to prove&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:56.160
strength. So here is the line that changes how&nbsp;
you watch a level. Don't watch where price breaks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.160 --> 00:13:02.800
Watch whether it can stay there. And here is the&nbsp;
measurable version so you are not guessing. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:10.480
a break, price must close beyond the level and&nbsp;
then build from it. Meaning the next candles hold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.480 --> 00:13:17.440
above and make higher lows. If price gives back&nbsp;
the entire break candle within three candles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:24.800
treat the attempt as failed and start looking the&nbsp;
other way. Three candles, whole candle given back.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.800 --> 00:13:31.520
That is the trigger. Now, four fast versions&nbsp;
to train your eyes. Break above the high. Snap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.520 --> 00:13:38.800
back. This is a 5-minute chart. Price clears the&nbsp;
morning high by a few ticks. One green candle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.800 --> 00:13:46.720
then several red candles back under it. Buyers&nbsp;
trapped and the next 15 minutes go straight down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:54.080
Same behavior, opposite direction. Daily chart.&nbsp;
Price breaks below a swing low, closes below it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.080 --> 00:14:00.960
and the following candle engulfves the entire&nbsp;
break and closes above the low. Sellers trapped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:07.440
and price runs for 2 weeks. Now, the version&nbsp;
that fools the most people. Big candle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:13.520
no continuation. Price rips through the level&nbsp;
with the biggest candle on the chart and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.520 --> 00:14:20.240
just stops. Four candles pass and none of them&nbsp;
make a new high. Price never came back through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:27.760
the level. So this is not a failure yet and it is&nbsp;
not a success either. Big candle plus no progress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:36.400
is a warning, not a signal. You wait and a slow&nbsp;
one because failure is not always dramatic. Price&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.400 --> 00:14:43.440
pushes above the level multiple times over an&nbsp;
hour. First push clears by a wide margin, then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.440 --> 00:14:50.560
returns. Second clears by less. Third barely pokes&nbsp;
through. The attack has clearly failed and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.560 --> 00:14:57.520
chart drops after the last attempt. One warning&nbsp;
so you don't turn this into a religion. A failed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.520 --> 00:15:04.880
break is a reason to get interested, not a reason&nbsp;
to click. Price can fail at a level and then just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.880 --> 00:15:11.840
sit there doing nothing for 2 hours. You need one&nbsp;
more thing and it is the next behavior. Compare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.840 --> 00:15:18.960
these two charts because both had a clean failed&nbsp;
break and only one of them was tradable chart on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:26.240
the left. Price breaks below the low, snaps back&nbsp;
above it, and then does nothing. Multiple candles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.240 --> 00:15:33.840
of overlapping bodies right at the failure area.&nbsp;
No push, no space, nothing. Chart on the right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:40.000
Same failed break below the low. Then look at the&nbsp;
next candles. This one is bigger than anything in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:47.200
the previous half hour. Then this one takes out&nbsp;
the last small high on the chart. So these candles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:54.240
travel farther. They overlap less and they cover&nbsp;
a bigger distance in fewer candles. That is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.240 --> 00:16:01.120
difference between price bouncing and price being&nbsp;
taken. So what happened here? Sellers pushed price&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.120 --> 00:16:08.400
down but failed. Then buyers pushed back, broke&nbsp;
the last small high and control changed hands.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:14.480
The evidence was the break of that last small high&nbsp;
because until price does that, the previous side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.480 --> 00:16:21.200
is still technically winning the sequence. Failure&nbsp;
shows you who is weak. The next break shows you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.200 --> 00:16:27.360
who is strong. Now a warm note for anyone&nbsp;
who has been trading for a few years because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:33.840
you have heard this under other names. This exact&nbsp;
sequence gets called a market structure shift or a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.840 --> 00:16:40.800
change of character and the failed breaks called a&nbsp;
liquidity sweep. The vocabulary is fine, but it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.800 --> 00:16:47.200
also why so many people can define the terms and&nbsp;
still cannot trade them live because they learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.200 --> 00:16:53.280
the label before they learned the behavior. But&nbsp;
you are doing it in the correct order. Here is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.280 --> 00:16:59.280
check. After the failed break, find the last&nbsp;
small swing point in the opposite direction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:07.440
If price closes beyond it, control has changed and&nbsp;
the chart goes live. If price stalls before it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.440 --> 00:17:14.320
there is no shift. And no matter how good the&nbsp;
failure looked, you stand down. And when control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.320 --> 00:17:21.280
does change, you still do not click. Look at where&nbsp;
price is right now on this chart. It is at the top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.280 --> 00:17:28.400
of a fast candle run with the nearest safe stop&nbsp;
sitting a long way below. The chart red is right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.400 --> 00:17:34.160
but your trade location is terrible. That&nbsp;
location problem has a specific solution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.160 --> 00:17:41.600
and it is where the actual trade lives. Watch this&nbsp;
trade happen live, and I will call every decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.600 --> 00:17:48.320
as it arrives. 15-minute chart. Price has spent&nbsp;
the morning pressing into the overnight high.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.320 --> 00:17:54.800
Pullback shrinking, ranges shrinking. It breaks&nbsp;
the high, gives the entire break candle back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.800 --> 00:18:02.880
in several candles, and then reverses hard and&nbsp;
closes below the last small swing low. Pressure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.880 --> 00:18:11.440
attempt, failure, control change. 405. The first&nbsp;
pullback creates or breaks the trade. You wait for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.440 --> 00:18:18.400
price to pull back up toward the failure area. The&nbsp;
character of that pullback decides everything. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:24.960
candles during the pullback should be smaller than&nbsp;
the shift candles. They should cover less range.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:32.960
The pace should feel slower and lazier compared to&nbsp;
the aggressive candles that establish new control.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.960 --> 00:18:40.080
If the pullback candles match the shift candles&nbsp;
in size, speed, and intensity, control has not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:47.120
truly changed and the setup breaks down. The ideal&nbsp;
pullback stalls near the area where the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.120 --> 00:18:54.960
failure happened. That zone acts as a floor on a&nbsp;
bullish setup or a ceiling on a bearish one. Look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.960 --> 00:19:02.160
price drifts toward it, test the area gently and&nbsp;
holds. When the next candle begins moving in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.160 --> 00:19:08.240
direction of new control with a strong body,&nbsp;
that is the entry. The stop sits just beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.240 --> 00:19:14.640
the failure zone. If price pushes back through&nbsp;
that area, the entire reading was wrong and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:14.640 --> 00:19:22.000
want a defined small loss for the target. Use the&nbsp;
next obvious area where price previously reacted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:28.800
or the next clean low in this bearish example.&nbsp;
So pressure leads into an attempt. The attempt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.800 --> 00:19:35.440
fails. The other side proves control. Then the&nbsp;
first week pullback gives us the entry. Here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.440 --> 00:19:42.400
is a bullish version. Price keeps testing a low.&nbsp;
Each bounce at the level is smaller, so pressure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.400 --> 00:19:48.880
is building. Sellers finally break below the&nbsp;
low. The break looks strong for one candle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.880 --> 00:19:56.320
but then price snaps back above it. Now buyers&nbsp;
respond and break the nearest small high. Price&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.320 --> 00:20:02.880
pulls back with several small red candles.&nbsp;
They move slowly and stop near the old low.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:02.880 --> 00:20:09.920
Then a strong green candle starts pushing higher&nbsp;
again. The stop goes under the failure and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.920 --> 00:20:16.480
first target is the next obvious high. Here's&nbsp;
the important filter. The first pullback is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.480 --> 00:20:22.240
usually more useful than the third or fourth one&nbsp;
because the failed side is still dealing with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.240 --> 00:20:28.800
mistake. Traders who entered the break are still&nbsp;
exiting. The market has just changed character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.800 --> 00:20:35.040
and the first return tests whether that change&nbsp;
can hold. So, if I miss the first clean return,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.040 --> 00:20:40.640
I don't need to chase the second move out of&nbsp;
frustration. Now, watch a setup that looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.640 --> 00:20:47.760
almost right. Pressure forms under a high. The&nbsp;
breakout fails. Sellers push lower and break a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:47.760 --> 00:20:55.520
small low. So far, everything is clean. One green&nbsp;
candle retraces half of the entire selling move.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:55.520 --> 00:21:01.600
The next candle pushes even higher with another&nbsp;
large body. The original selling looked strong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:01.600 --> 00:21:08.880
but buyers are now matching that strength almost&nbsp;
immediately. I don't want to sell into that. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.880 --> 00:21:15.360
gives us a practical decision rule. I want a&nbsp;
clear failure, a real control change, a weaker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:15.360 --> 00:21:21.120
first pullback, and [clears throat] enough room&nbsp;
to the next obstacle to justify the risk. When one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.120 --> 00:21:28.160
of them is missing, the best decision is often no&nbsp;
trade. Now, learning to reject the setup protects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.160 --> 00:21:34.880
more capital over time than any entry technique.&nbsp;
So, let's build a filter that will keep you out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:34.880 --> 00:21:40.880
weak trades. A stronger pattern has qualities&nbsp;
you can check in seconds. The level should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.880 --> 00:21:47.200
obvious to anyone looking at the chart without&nbsp;
extra lines or annotations. If you need to draw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.200 --> 00:21:53.440
additional tools or convince yourself the level&nbsp;
is real, it's probably not clean enough to build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:53.440 --> 00:22:00.000
a trade on. Pressure should be visible without&nbsp;
assistance. Meaning the pullbacks shrink and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:06.160
candles compress, overlap, have wicks on both&nbsp;
sides, and you can point to the tightening with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.160 --> 00:22:12.960
your finger on the screen. The failure should be&nbsp;
unmistakable, a clear push through the level that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:12.960 --> 00:22:18.800
was immediately rejected by price snapping back.&nbsp;
The shift in the opposite direction should look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:18.800 --> 00:22:25.040
noticeably more aggressive than the candles that&nbsp;
preceded it. And the first pullback should appear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:25.040 --> 00:22:31.760
weak and tired compared to the shift. A weaker&nbsp;
pattern looks different in every respect. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.760 --> 00:22:38.800
candles sit in the middle of a wide range with no&nbsp;
clear level nearby creating friction. There is no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:38.800 --> 00:22:46.400
visible pressure because price bounces randomly in&nbsp;
both directions with similarly sized candles and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:46.400 --> 00:22:53.360
no tightening. No obvious failure occurs because&nbsp;
price never committed strongly enough to one side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:53.360 --> 00:22:59.840
to trap anyone. These charts contain noise and&nbsp;
the correct decision is to close them and look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:59.840 --> 00:23:07.520
elsewhere. Here is a clean example. The level&nbsp;
is obvious, a high that price tested two times.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:07.520 --> 00:23:14.160
Pressure is visible through progressively smaller&nbsp;
pullbacks and shrinking candles. The failure is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:14.160 --> 00:23:22.160
sharp with one candle above the high immediately&nbsp;
reversed by strong candles closing back below. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:22.160 --> 00:23:29.360
shift covers more ground than any candle in the&nbsp;
prior range. The pullback retraces a small portion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:29.360 --> 00:23:36.560
of the shift with several smaller candles. Every&nbsp;
piece of this story tells the same thing. Here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.560 --> 00:23:43.520
is an almost valid version. The level exists and&nbsp;
some compression is present, but the failure is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:43.520 --> 00:23:50.960
shallow. One small candle crossed the level before&nbsp;
drifting back and the shift candles are a mix of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:50.960 --> 00:23:57.920
strong and indecisive bars. This could produce&nbsp;
a move, but the reading is not clean. Skipping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:57.920 --> 00:24:05.040
it costs you nothing. Forcing it risks a loss&nbsp;
built on ambiguous context. There is one optional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:05.040 --> 00:24:11.440
check that helps on the failed attempt and it&nbsp;
is free. You check the volume on the strongest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:11.440 --> 00:24:18.160
failed brakes. The brake handle carries the less&nbsp;
volume than before and the candle that reverses it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:18.160 --> 00:24:24.960
carries higher volume. That combination means&nbsp;
a crowd got in and then got flushed. If the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:24.960 --> 00:24:31.600
break comes on thin volume and reverses on thin&nbsp;
volume, the pattern still works. You just need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:31.600 --> 00:24:38.480
read the volume after the first pullback. That's&nbsp;
because low volume on failure and low volume on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:38.480 --> 00:24:45.440
control change means fewer traders are trapped and&nbsp;
there is less fuel for the move away. Don't turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:45.440 --> 00:24:52.560
that into a requirement. Volume is just a second&nbsp;
opinion in case things are unclear. Now pattern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:52.560 --> 00:24:57.920
recognition doesn't develop from watching.&nbsp;
It develops from predicting and checking the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:57.920 --> 00:25:05.520
result. So, open Trading View and go into replay&nbsp;
mode. Here's the exact routine. Choose one market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:05.520 --> 00:25:12.320
you already know and one time frame you can read&nbsp;
comfortably. Move the replay point back until the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:12.320 --> 00:25:20.080
future candles disappear. Then find price near a&nbsp;
clear high or low and pause. Where is the fight?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:20.080 --> 00:25:26.800
Who is attacking it? Then make one prediction&nbsp;
before you reveal. Will the next serious attempt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:26.800 --> 00:25:34.240
hold or will it fail? Then advance a few candles.&nbsp;
If price breaks and holds, study what allowed it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:34.240 --> 00:25:40.400
to keep the new ground. Did the breakout create&nbsp;
distance immediately? Did the first pullback stay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:40.400 --> 00:25:47.440
above the old high? Did the opposite side fail&nbsp;
to produce any real response? If the break fails,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:47.440 --> 00:25:54.000
mark the candle where price returns through&nbsp;
the level. Save both kinds of examples. Under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:54.000 --> 00:26:01.120
each screenshot, write one plain sentence about&nbsp;
what happened. Something like large breakout,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:01.120 --> 00:26:07.600
no follow through, immediate close back inside.&nbsp;
You are giving your brain a simple description to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:07.600 --> 00:26:14.560
attach to the visual memory. After 10 examples,&nbsp;
place the successful breaks beside the failed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:14.560 --> 00:26:21.680
ones. Don't compare their pattern names, compare&nbsp;
their behavior. That is where small differences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:21.680 --> 00:26:29.520
become much easier to notice. Now test yourself&nbsp;
with several unlabeled charts. First one, price&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:29.520 --> 00:26:36.640
has tested this low three times. The first bounce&nbsp;
is large. The second is smaller. The third stays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:36.640 --> 00:26:43.680
close to the level. What is happening? Pressure is&nbsp;
building. Now we advance the chart. Sellers break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:43.680 --> 00:26:50.240
the low with a strong candle. But the next candle&nbsp;
prints a long lower wick and closes back above&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:50.240 --> 00:26:57.760
it. The attempt failed. Advance again. Two strong&nbsp;
green candles push higher and break the last small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:57.760 --> 00:27:05.680
high. Control changes. Then price pulls back with&nbsp;
several red candles stops near the old low and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:05.680 --> 00:27:12.560
green candle starts expanding higher. That is the&nbsp;
first pullback trade. Notice how you never had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:12.560 --> 00:27:19.600
predict the entire future from the first frame.&nbsp;
Each event gave you one new piece of information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:19.600 --> 00:27:26.240
and your decision updated with it. Second one,&nbsp;
price presses into a high. There's are a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:26.240 --> 00:27:32.720
overlapping candles just below the level. Then&nbsp;
a large green candle breaks above the level and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:32.720 --> 00:27:40.480
closes near its top. Would you call this a failed&nbsp;
breakout? Not yet. Now price continues higher and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:40.480 --> 00:27:47.760
the first pullback stays above the old high. The&nbsp;
attack worked. This is a continuation trade, not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:47.760 --> 00:27:56.000
a failed break reversal. Another one. Price breaks&nbsp;
below a low, snaps back above it, and buyers push&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:56.000 --> 00:28:02.720
through a small high. So far, the reversal looks&nbsp;
clean. Then the pullback starts with one large red&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:02.720 --> 00:28:10.160
candle that erases most of the buyer shift. Would&nbsp;
you take the long? I would not. The return is too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:10.160 --> 00:28:17.280
strong relative to the move that was supposed to&nbsp;
prove buyer control. Price may still rally later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:17.280 --> 00:28:24.080
but this entry no longer gives me the clean&nbsp;
relationship I wanted. This one, price compresses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:24.080 --> 00:28:31.440
under a high, then it breaks it, rejects it, and&nbsp;
moves below our zone of interest. Then it stalls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:31.440 --> 00:28:39.120
there and moves back up slightly. Do you take the&nbsp;
short? Look closer. Sellers never break the minor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:39.120 --> 00:28:46.560
higher low. We didn't have a change in control.&nbsp;
This one is a pass. We had pressure attempt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:46.560 --> 00:28:53.200
but not the control shift. It's easy. Actually,&nbsp;
if you repeat this simple routine daily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:53.200 --> 00:28:59.280
you'll soon spot the sequence forming in real&nbsp;
time. The more examples your brain processes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:59.280 --> 00:29:06.080
and files away, the less time each new chart&nbsp;
demands, find the level, watch the attempt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:06.080 --> 00:29:12.320
notice the failure or acceptance, and follow&nbsp;
the new control after the first pullback.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:12.320 --> 00:29:18.640
But there is one piece of information we have&nbsp;
not fully used yet. How much participation sits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:18.640 --> 00:29:26.640
behind each move? We talked briefly about volume&nbsp;
as a confirmation tool when setups are 50/50. Two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:26.640 --> 00:29:32.880
candles can look almost identical while one&nbsp;
has real pressure behind it and the other is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:32.880 --> 00:29:39.600
running out of fuel. And once you learn to read&nbsp;
volume, failed breakouts, reversals, and strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:39.600 --> 00:29:46.880
continuations become much easier to separate. That&nbsp;
is exactly what I break down in the next video.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:46.880 --> 00:29:53.200
And if you're ready to fit this into a full a toz&nbsp;
trading system, that's what our academy is for.

